I now have a complete, precise understanding of the bug and exactly what needs to change. Here is the full analysis and fix.

---

## Root Cause Analysis

The bug lives entirely in **`src/services/user_service.hpp`**, in the `loginWithSocial` method (lines 186–206). Here is the faulty logic, annotated:

```cpp
AuthResult loginWithSocial(domain::ports::OAuthProvider provider,
                           const std::string&           accessToken) const
{
    auto profile = m_oauthGateway->fetchProfile(provider, accessToken);
    if (!profile)
        throw AuthenticationError{"Unable to fetch social profile"};

    // ❶ Only looks up the user by (provider, provider-specific-id)
    auto user = m_userRepo->findByOauth(provider, profile->id);
    if (!user)
    {
        // ❷ If not found by OAuth identity, ALWAYS creates a brand-new user —
        //    never checks whether the email already belongs to an existing account.
        user.emplace(domain::entities::User::createSocial(
            provider,
            profile->id,
            profile->email,
            profile->displayName));
        m_userRepo->save(*user);   // ← duplicate user record created here
    }

    return generateAuthResult(*user);
}
```

**The two-step lookup is missing its second step.** When `findByOauth(provider, profile->id)` returns `std::nullopt` (which it always will the first time a user tries a new provider, or when using `GenericOAuth` for the first time), the code unconditionally creates a new user. It never asks: *"Does a user with this email address already exist?"* — so a user who registered with a password, or with Google, and then tries `GenericOAuth` with the same email gets a second, empty account.

---

## Exact Changes Required

### File: `src/services/user_service.hpp`

**Function: `loginWithSocial`** (lines 186–206)

The fix requires a **three-step lookup** instead of two:

1. Look up by `(provider, provider_id)` — the fast path for returning social users. ✅ (already present)
2. **NEW:** If not found, look up by `profile->email` via `findByEmail`. If an existing account is found, **link** the new OAuth identity to it (update the record) and return that account — do **not** create a new one.
3. Only if neither lookup finds anything, provision a genuinely new account. ✅ (already present, just needs to be guarded by step 2)

```cpp
[[nodiscard]]
AuthResult loginWithSocial(domain::ports::OAuthProvider provider,
                           const std::string&           accessToken) const
{
    auto profile = m_oauthGateway->fetchProfile(provider, accessToken);
    if (!profile)
        throw AuthenticationError{"Unable to fetch social profile"};

    // Step 1: fast path — user has logged in with this exact provider before.
    auto user = m_userRepo->findByOauth(provider, profile->id);

    if (!user && !profile->email.empty())
    {
        // Step 2: email-based merge — the user exists under a different
        // credential (local password, another social provider, etc.).
        user = m_userRepo->findByEmail(profile->email);
        if (user)
        {
            // Link this OAuth identity to the existing account so that
            // Step 1 succeeds on every subsequent login.
            user->linkOAuthIdentity(provider, profile->id);
            m_userRepo->save(*user);
        }
    }

    if (!user)
    {
        // Step 3: genuinely new user — provision a fresh account.
        user.emplace(domain::entities::User::createSocial(
            provider,
            profile->id,
            profile->email,
            profile->displayName));
        m_userRepo->save(*user);
    }

    return generateAuthResult(*user);
}
```

The key additions are:
- **The `if (!user && !profile->email.empty())` block** — only runs when the OAuth-identity lookup missed, and only when the provider actually returned an email (guarding against providers that omit it).
- **`m_userRepo->findByEmail(profile->email)`** — reuses the already-present `findByEmail` port method (used by `registerLocal` and `loginLocal`) to find the pre-existing account.
- **`user->linkOAuthIdentity(provider, profile->id)`** — records the new OAuth identity on the existing `User` entity so that the next login goes through the fast path (Step 1) and avoids repeating the email lookup every time.
- **`m_userRepo->save(*user)`** — persists the newly linked identity.

---

### File: `tests/test_user_service.cpp`

**Function / test case: `"UserService – social login"`** (lines 215–239)

A new `SECTION` must be added to cover the account-merge scenario. The existing `InMemoryUserRepository` test double already implements `findByEmail`, so only the test logic needs to be added:

```cpp
SECTION("social login with existing email merges into existing account, not a new one")
{
    // Arrange: register a local user with the same email the OAuth provider returns.
    // DummyOAuthProvider always returns "luna@oauth.test".
    service.registerUser({ "luna@oauth.test", "P@ssw0rd1", "Luna Local" });
    REQUIRE(repo->size() == 1);  // one account exists

    // Act: social login with the same email address.
    AuthResult result = service.socialLogin("VALID_CODE");

    // Assert: no new account was created — still exactly one user record.
    REQUIRE(repo->size() == 1);
    REQUIRE(result.user.email() == "luna@oauth.test");

    // A second social login must also succeed (OAuth identity is now linked).
    AuthResult result2 = service.socialLogin("VALID_CODE");
    REQUIRE(repo->size() == 1);
}
```

This test would **fail** against the current code (because `repo->size()` would be 2 after the social login) and **pass** after the fix.

---

## Summary Table

| File | Location | What to change |
|---|---|---|
| `src/services/user_service.hpp` | `UserService::loginWithSocial` (lines 193–203) | Insert a second lookup by `profile->email` via `m_userRepo->findByEmail()` between the `findByOauth` miss and the new-user creation block; if found, call `user->linkOAuthIdentity(provider, profile->id)` and `m_userRepo->save(*user)` to merge and persist the link. |
| `tests/test_user_service.cpp` | `"UserService – social login"` test case (after line 233) | Add a `SECTION` that pre-registers a local user with the same email the `DummyOAuthProvider` returns, then asserts that `socialLogin` does **not** create a second record (`repo->size() == 1`). |
